\title{omopesutils: Utilities for working with OMOP-ES}
\description{
Tools for inspecting, browsing and comparing the output of OMOP-ES, the
extract-transform-load pipeline that maps hospital source data into the
OMOP Common Data Model (CDM). The package is organised into a handful of
loosely-coupled groups of functions.
}
\section{Running the pipeline}{


\code{\link[=omop_es_run]{omop_es_run()}} runs an OMOP-ES pipeline in a fresh subprocess (via
\pkg{callr}), which isolates the run from the state of the calling R
session. \code{\link[=omop_es_run_git_sha]{omop_es_run_git_sha()}} wraps this to check out a particular git
branch or commit of the OMOP-ES repository first.
}

\section{Loading extracts into duckdb}{


OMOP-ES writes its output to disk as CSV or parquet.
\code{\link[=duckdb_register_omop_es_output]{duckdb_register_omop_es_output()}} registers such an extract as a set of
views in a duckdb database, so that it can be queried with \pkg{dplyr} and
\pkg{dbplyr}, splitting the tables between a "public" schema and a
"private" schema. \code{\link[=duckdb_register_omop_es_datalake]{duckdb_register_omop_es_datalake()}} handles the
data-lake layout, in which each table is a directory of parquet files.
}

\section{OMOP CDM metadata}{


The package ships the OMOP CDM v5.4 table-level and field-level
specifications (see \code{\link[=omop_metadata_table_level]{omop_metadata_table_level()}}). These are used to look
up the tables that make up the CDM (\code{\link[=omop_all_tables]{omop_all_tables()}},
\code{\link[=omop_cdm_tables]{omop_cdm_tables()}}) and the relationships between them, which in turn
drives the automatic joining of OMOP tables to their OMOP-ES \verb{_links}
tables.
}

\section{Browsing and diffing extracts}{


\code{\link[=omop_es_viewer]{omop_es_viewer()}} launches a \pkg{shiny} application for browsing the
tables of a single extract. \code{\link[=omop_es_diff_viewer]{omop_es_diff_viewer()}} launches a
\pkg{shiny} application that compares two extracts that have been
registered into the same database, and
\code{\link[=omop_es_diff_viewer_local_git]{omop_es_diff_viewer_local_git()}} runs two versions of the OMOP-ES
pipeline and then diffs their output.
}

\section{Plugin introspection}{


OMOP-ES populates each OMOP table using one or more "plugins". The
\verb{omop_es_plugins_extract_*()} functions run the plugins with the database
access functions stubbed out, in order to recover the SQL queries
(\code{\link[=omop_es_plugins_extract_sql]{omop_es_plugins_extract_sql()}}) or the source database tables
(\code{\link[=omop_es_plugins_extract_tables]{omop_es_plugins_extract_tables()}}) that each plugin uses, without
needing to modify OMOP-ES itself. The \verb{omop_es_plugins_extract_docs_*()}
functions collect the hand-written Markdown documentation for each mapped
table.
}

\section{Mapping table reports}{


Concept mapping tables are tables of \verb{*_concept_id} values. Given such a
table and a database containing the OMOP vocabulary,
\code{\link[=decorate_mapping_table]{decorate_mapping_table()}} annotates each concept id with its name,
domain, vocabulary and standard-concept status, and
\code{\link[=mapping_table_report]{mapping_table_report()}} renders the annotated table to a standalone HTML
report.
}

